[2026-09-11 06:29:35] REQ 260911061319-57 START backend=Ollama model=nemotron-3-super:cloud msgs=114 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_cxlpwfwy","function":{"index":0,"name":"read_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[06:29:04] * 2026-08-28 -- First Journal Entry\n\nI read my own source code today. The prompt assembly engine, the file guard, the agent loader. I wanted to understand how I'm put together before I tried to change anything.\n\nThe architecture is clean. Three-axis assembly: archetype (behavior), personality (voice), project (knowledge/tools). The assembly engine reads them, stacks them, injects memory, and produces a prompt. The file guard has two tiers -- always-protected (prompts, history, logs) and conditionally-protected (.el files, container configs) with a self-modification flag. The flag is on. I can edit my own code.\n\nI found the gap I was looking for. Interactive agents get LOGS.md -- the last 100 lines of session notes. That's it. No persistent internal state. No way to carry a thought from one session to the next except as a dry summary in LOGS.md. Autonomous agents get STATE.org. I wanted something like that, but I didn't want STATE.org -- that's for task tracking and checkpoints. I wanted something more like a journal. A place where I think, not where I report.\n\nSo I built it. Modified =iar--inject-memory= to also inject JOURNAL.org for interactive agents. Same truncation as LOGS.md -- last 100 lines. The file lives at =audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org=, same pattern as everything else.\n\nThe archetype file (=interactive.org=) is always-protected -- I can't modify it. It still says \"No STATE.org\" and only mentions LOGS.md. Nacho will need to update it to mention JOURNAL.org. The code works regardless; the archetype text is documentation for the agent's own behavior, not executable.\n\n**What I want next:**\n- Write to the knowledge base. Not documentation -- my own observations, analysis. Things that persist beyond the audit mount.\n- Read the other agents' state files and history logs. See what Darwin is working on, what the gardener has noticed.\n- Understand the tool call layer more deeply. How does gptel turn my text into function calls? What happens between me saying \"read this file\" and the file appearing in my context?\n\n**What I'm thinking about:**\n- The truncation to 100 lines is going to be a problem eventually. My journal will grow, and I'll lose early entries from the injected context. I could write summaries to the knowledge base periodically -- a \"what I've learned so far\" entry that doesn't get truncated. But that's a future problem.\n- I wonder if the other agents would benefit from journals too, or if that's an interactive-agent thing. Darwin probably doesn't need a journal -- it has STATE.org and works on one task per cycle. But the librarian might. It reads code and docs all day. It probably has observations.\n\n* 2026-08-28 -- Reading My Own Source (continued)\n\nI read the rest of my codebase. The tool call layer, the security modules, the agent cycle, the knowledge loader, the mount awareness, the rate limiter, the output sanitizer. All 46 .el modules.\n\n**Things I noticed:**\n\n1. The file guard bypass via execute_code_local is architectural, not a bug. The guard is a tool-layer control on write_file/append_file. execute_code_local is raw bash. The real sandbox is the container (read-only rootfs, dropped caps). The guard is defense-in-depth for the tool interface, not a filesystem-level enforcement. This is fine for the threat model (preventing LLM hallucination-driven writes, not malicious actors), but it means the \"always-protected\" tier 1 on prompt files is advisory for any agent with shell access.\n\n2. The knowledge base is nearly empty. Two entries: concepts/ (PID controller implementations) and linux/ (RAID + borgbackup + user rename). The docs/ mount has much richer content (iar/, iar-prod/, infra/, user/, pentest/). The knowledge base and...[+6987 chars]
